# This project owns its pixi manifest, so undo the global ignores set in
# ~/dotfiles/home/shell.nix (programs.git.ignores). Those exist to stop pixi files from
# ad-hoc directories being committed by accident; a project whose dependencies and lock
# file ARE the reproducibility story has to track both, and pixi's own guidance says so.
# Without this the repo cannot be cloned and run, and a worktree has no manifest at all --
# which is how it was noticed.
!pixi.toml
!pixi.lock

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pixi/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
__marimo__/

# Derived caches, not measurements. The observer's log-likelihood grid is recomputed from
# calibration-responses.jsonl whenever it is absent (see notebooks/vision.py), so tracking
# it would put a 28 MB binary in history that nothing can diff and every clone must fetch.
# The measurements themselves ARE tracked: small, append-only, one JSON object per line,
# and a real diff when they change.
data/observer-logp-*.npy

# The published champion is a RESULT, not a record: `pixi run publish` (or any pass of the
# analysis notebook) rewrites it from the two response logs in seconds. Tracking it made
# `git status` dirty on every reading. What IS tracked is the event of applying one --
# data/applied-themes.jsonl carries each applied palette with its provenance.
data/measured-theme.json
